# TortOS's inittab: the base system's (board/px2/rootfs-overlay/etc/inittab)
# with the splash first and TortOS in place of the cube and powerkey. Keep
# the two in step.
#
# The picture comes first: the splash needs only /dev, which the kernel mounts
# itself, and everything below it would otherwise run before it. There's
# no remount of / (the kernel mounts it read-write already, and doing it
# again cost 90 ms), no swapon (there is no swap), and no getty on tty1
# (with the kernel console off, nothing on screen would show it).

::sysinit:/etc/init.d/splash start
::sysinit:/bin/mount -t proc proc /proc
::sysinit:/bin/mkdir -p /dev/pts /dev/shm
::sysinit:/bin/mount -a
# The card's games partition, then TortOS from it, ahead of rcS: TortOS needs
# /proc, /tmp and the card and nothing else rcS starts, so it starts here, in
# the background, and the rest of the boot carries on beside it. It takes the
# display from the splash and handles the power button itself.
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/datapart start
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/tortos start
::sysinit:/bin/mkdir -p /run/lock/subsys
null::sysinit:/bin/ln -sf /proc/self/fd /dev/fd
null::sysinit:/bin/ln -sf /proc/self/fd/0 /dev/stdin
null::sysinit:/bin/ln -sf /proc/self/fd/1 /dev/stdout
null::sysinit:/bin/ln -sf /proc/self/fd/2 /dev/stderr
::sysinit:/bin/hostname -F /etc/hostname
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS

::respawn:/usr/sbin/jackswitch

# Busybox runs these before it kills the remaining processes, so stop them
# here first: anything still holding a file open for writing (Mesa's shader
# cache, a game's save) made the read-only remount fail, and every next boot
# then replayed the ext4 journal, 400 ms of mounting root. Diatom saves the
# running game on SIGTERM. The card's partition is unmounted on its own,
# waiting for the programs that run from it to go (see datapart).
::shutdown:/etc/init.d/rcK
::shutdown:/bin/kill -TERM -1
::shutdown:/bin/usleep 200000
::shutdown:/etc/init.d/datapart stop
::shutdown:/bin/umount -a -r
